I don't necessarily think the queen was  a style icon because she didn't dress to  be emulated and copied but she had this  Timeless sense of style so she was  always I suppose in fashion given the  fact that she was always carrying out  engagements and you know a very busy  working woman as Queen and you know the  Wardrobe was really important so she  didn't ever want hemlines to be too  short and she didn't want things to be  showing too much flesh she needed  certain Fabrics that wouldn't crease  when she was getting in and out of the  car and she loved color so she would  never wear black unless she was in  mourning for the queen it was all about  being seen and often not heard but the  colors of her outfits and what she wore  was really important the queen hated  anything that was flimsy so she liked  weighty clothes you know clothes that  hung beautifully clothes that were  beautifully tailored she loved walls and  you know heavier fabrics and there was a  really practical reason to not wanting  to wear floaty dresses and remember in  her youth she had the most beautiful  figure she was absolutely gorgeous but  she hated the idea of perhaps coming  down a plane or stepping out into a gust  of wind and her skirts lifting up so she  very cleverly used to have her dresser  stitch in weights into the hems of her  skirt lines to keep those skirts hanging  down in the right way and to stop them  from lifting up so I think it was always  common sense and practicality above  style but she was just effortlessly  stylish I think quite a lot of the  Royals have learned their lessons  actually I always remember being with  the Duchess of Cambridge as she then was  Kate we'd arrived in Calgary in Canada  for an engagement and as she was on the  tarmac the wind came up and The Duchess  he was wearing this beautiful yellow  dress didn't know what to do with  herself because she was greeting a  little girl and as she did her dress  just flew up I think those photos have  not quite seen the light of day but it  just goes to show that when it comes to  Royal dressing the queen really got it  right with practicality over and above  style you know she loved her tartans she  loved her Classics she didn't really  depart too much from what she knew  worked for her so if you look back at  her wardrobe the dress coat is a staple  of the royal wardrobe Day dresses you  rarely saw her in trousers apart from  when she was riding her horse Elizabeth  never set out to be a style icon but  because she came to the throne So Young  when she was in her mid-20s 25. she was  absolutely exquisitely beautiful as a  young Queen I think we forget now but  she was So Glamorous she would turn up  at State dinners and receptions wearing  fur stalls and dripping with diamonds  and just looking absolutely Exquisite  you know if you were lucky enough to be  with her on the Dance Floor she would  often wear an off-the-shoulder dress and  her fur stalls and look really like a  movie star since she would play with  color she loved color and she'd  experiment with patterns as well and I  and I certainly think in her later years  she was never afraid to wear strong  colors I always remember her at her 90th  birthday celebration since she came out  from the Buckingham Palace balcony  wearing neon green and we all christened  her on neon Queen but it was remarkable  she could pull off neon at 90. I think  because the queen was a woman of very  few words let's not forget she never  actually gave an interview we never  really knew what she felt about certain  things she used her wardrobe as a way of  expressing herself the queen often used  her wardrobe to convey messages where  often she wasn't able to say anything  when she arrived in Canada more than  giving her sort of an opening speech as  soon as she stepped off the plane there  wearing red and white or the maple  brooch that the people of Canada had  gifted her more often than not she would  speak but that outfit said it all it was  her way of paying tribute to the country  that she was visiting and then when she  visited Ireland she wore green which was  the color of Ireland and that was just  an immediate symbol of peace love and  respect for Ireland and I think from  that moment on the tour just went  perfectly the queen of course has an  extensive jewelry collection and a lot  of that is jewelry that has been gifted  to her so the people of New Zealand have  given her a diamond Fern roach which she  would always wear when she went to New  Zealand the people of Canada gave her  the maple leaf also in diamonds and  what's so lovely has been to see the  queen loan that jewelry to other  Generations so we saw Kate wearing the  maple brooch and the judge inches of  Cornwall wearing pieces of jewelry that  the queen also wore to those Nations so  they have sentimental value the queen  used jewelry to send out a message a  message of love and affection for the  nation that she was visiting and in a  lovely sort of gesture handed it down  the generation so that the Duchess of  Cornwall The Duchess of Cambridge could  do the same thing the queen actually I  think was most at home when she was at  Balmoral or she was at Windsor and she  was riding out and so you know you would  see the queen in in jodhpurs and a  riding jacket but she did love a Tweed  skirt and even when she was up walking  in the highlands in Balmoral it would be  boots always muddy and always flat  teamed with a Tweed skirt and she you  know she believed in wrapping up for the  weather whether it was rain or shine  she'd always have her rain Mark and her  dog leads ready by the door to go out  but she did love being casual I think  that's where she felt most relaxed I  think one of the most iconic things  about the queen was her handbag she  always carried a Lorna handbag bag and  they carry the moral warrant it was it  will I think it will forever be the bag  that is synonymous with the queen she  kept very little in it packet of mints a  crisply folded five pound note a compact  mirror and her lipstick but she was  never without that bag and that bag  served another really useful purpose  other than finishing off her outfit  beautifully she would use that bag to  communicate messages if the bag moved  from one hand to another it meant the  queen wanted to be moved on whoever she  was speaking to was deeply dull if she  put the bag on the floor it meant she  wanted to get out of that situation  immediately so the bag was really  fundamental in her conveying messages to  her staff  [Music]
 

